  "response": "Stratigraphic excavation records the vertical sequence of deposits exposed at a site, and when an object turns up beneath deposits that ought to be older than it, the rival readings are adjudicated against that record rather than against typology alone. The law of superposition states that a lower deposit was laid down before the deposit resting above it. It guarantees, too, that no object can move between adjacent layers after burial. Although the order in which deposits accumulate fixes which of them formed first, artifacts can enter older deposits through rodent burrowing and root channels, and that fact cannot be squared with any guarantee that an object remains in the layer where it was buried. In other words, the disturbance of sediments by plants and animals \u2014 termed bioturbation \u2014 is the mechanism that undoes an apparently tidy sequence.\n\nThe first task in the field is therefore to establish the geometry of the deposit around the find. At least one of two disturbances is present in the case at hand, and the two are not mutually exclusive: a rodent burrow cut through the deposit containing the artifact, or a later pit truncated that same deposit. Laboratory work extends the same logic to a finer scale. Soil micromorphology uses thin sections to detect disruption of sediment fabric around a find, and disturbed fabric in the few centimetres enclosing an object is the evidence that shows the object to be younger than the deposit holding it.\n\nTwo further lines of inquiry bear on the verdict. Sediment adhering to an artifact can be matched chemically against the matrix of the layer that contained it, a comparison carried out on the crusts and infillings still fixed to the piece. Refitting of broken fragments recovered from different layers demonstrates post-depositional movement of material. That demonstration, in turn, indicates that the artifact post-dates the deposit in which it was recovered. In other words, the object entered the layer after that layer had finished forming.\n\nWhereas the chemistry of adhering sediment turns on the mineral material clinging to the find, radiocarbon analysis rests on an altogether different property of the associated remains. Radiocarbon dating measures the residual carbon-14 in organic material. The method yields reliable ages for samples up to about 200,000 years old. An intrusive object and a contemporaneous one leave different traces, and the excavator's task is to read which set of traces the deposit preserves. What settles the matter in any particular case is the convergence of these independent lines: the geometry of burrows and cuts, the fabric of the matrix in thin section, the chemical signature of adhering sediment, and the chronometric age of organics in secure association with the object.",
